  "brief_md": "# Evidence Brief: A16 — Local CI Runner with Step-Through Debugging\n\n## Idea\nA local runner that replicates the CI environment with step-through debugging so developers stop committing to test pipeline YAML blind.\n\n## Payer\nIndividual developers/DevOps engineers, expensing a personal dev tool. Price hypothesis: ~$15-20/month individual, ~$200/month team tier, anchored to Depot's published $20/month single-developer CI plan ([source](https://bex.co/blog/2026/08/29/depot-blacksmith-ci-runner-pricing-self-hosted-build-pool)). No exact paid comparable exists in this specific category — every direct competitor found is free.\n\n## Competitors\nThe space is dense and entirely free/open-source:\n- **nektos/act** — 72k stars, free, OSS ([link](https://github.com/nektos/act))\n- **redwoodjs/local-ci** — free dev tool ([link](https://github.com/redwoodjs/local-ci))\n- **actdbg** — free PyPI package, explicit step-through debugger for GitHub Actions ([link](https://pypi.org/project/actdbg/))\n- **PipeStep** — free, MIT, 20 stars, Docker-based step debugger ([link](https://github.com/Photobombastic/pipestep))\n- **GitLab's native debugging tools** — included free with all GitLab tiers ([link](https://docs.gitlab.com/ci/debugging/))\n- **walkflow** and **ciwalk** — two more free clones of this exact idea, both shipped by solo hobbyists in mid-to-late 2026 ([walkflow](https://github.com/mrvlyouknowwho/walkflow), [ciwalk](https://github.com/kiwi-07/ciwalk))\n- **getlocalci/local-ci** — free VS Code extension, CircleCI-specific ([link](https://github.com/getlocalci/local-ci))\n\n## Acquisition Channel\n- Developer newsletter sponsorship: entry tier ~$380/issue (daily.dev), scaling to $15-18k for top-tier newsletters like TLDR ([source](https://business.daily.dev/resources/best-developer-newsletters-to-sponsor/)).\n- Organic HN/Reddit/dev.to launch posts: $0 cash cost; this is literally how every existing competitor in this space has been distributed, e.g. the walkflow dev.to launch post ([source](https://dev.to/mrvlyouknowwho/push-and-pray-ci-is-dumb-i-built-a-step-debugger-for-github-actions-15an)).\n\n## Demand Evidence\n- \"I tried for many moons to replicate GHA environment on local and it's impossible in my context... It's like a slot machine.\" ([HN](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=43420462))\n- \"after way too many 'push and pray' debugging sessions the solution was just wrapping everything behind a makefile\" ([Reddit](https://www.reddit.com/r/programming/comments/1s5ja3a/github_actions_is_slowly_killing_your_engineering/))\n- \"One-line YAML fix. Push. Wait 4 minutes... Repeat 14 times... that's roughly $43,000/year you're paying engineers to wait on YAML.\" ([LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/paoloperrone_yesterday-i-lost-an-entire-afternoon-to-github-activity-7478193198276902912-4GA8))\n- Seed quote: \"I would love to be able to have access to the same env as the CI... Solve this and I would pay for it.\" ([HN](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46345827))\n\n## Case For\nThe pain is well-documented and recurring across multiple independent sources over several years, with people quantifying real dollar costs. The build is technically tractable for a solo founder with AI agents — proven by multiple hobbyists already shipping working MVPs in days to weeks. Existing tools are early-stage and unpolished, leaving room to differentiate on UX, multi-provider support, and paid team features.\n\n## Case Against\nAt least seven free, open-source competitors already exist, several launched within the same few months in 2026, showing the idea is both in-demand and trivially cloneable — with zero price validation in the category (every found competitor is free). True environment parity is inherently hard (self-hosted runners, GPU/ARM, secrets, network services), capping the achievable value. CI platform vendors have the incentive and distribution to ship this natively (GitLab already does, partially), risking commoditization. The buyer is an individual developer, historically one of the hardest audiences to convert from free OSS tools to paid subscriptions without an enterprise upsell layer.\n\n## Open Questions\n- Would developers pay given ~7 free alternatives already exist, and for which specific added feature?\n- How far can local env parity realistically go for complex CI setups?\n- Is there a moat given how fast competitors have been cloned?\n- Would a platform-native feature (e.g., GitLab's) eliminate the market opportunity?\n- Would a team-focused hosted SaaS be a stronger wedge than a free-equivalent local CLI?\n"
